{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"function-templates","title":"Function Templates","group":"Templates & Generic Programming","order":42,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A template is a blueprint the compiler stamps out for each set of types you use it with. Write the algorithm once; get type-checked, fully optimised code for every type.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Defining and calling</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;                     // `class T` means the same\nT maxOf(T a, T b) { return a &gt; b ? a : b; }\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T, typename U&gt;\nauto add(T a, U b) { return a + b; }      // return type deduced from the expression\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T, int N&gt;              // non-type template parameter\nconstexpr int length(const T (&amp;)[N]) { return N; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; maxOf(3, 7) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; maxOf(2.5, 1.5) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; maxOf&lt;std::string&gt;(\"pear\", \"apple\") &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; add(1, 2.5) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; length(arr) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // maxOf(3, 7.0);   // error: T deduced as both int and double\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; maxOf&lt;double&gt;(3, 7.5) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // explicit template argument\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7 2.5 pear\n3.5\n4\n7.5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>How instantiation works</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Deduction</div><div class=\"step-desc\">From the call <code>maxOf(3, 7)</code> the compiler deduces <code>T = int</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Instantiation</div><div class=\"step-desc\">It generates <code>int maxOf&lt;int&gt;(int, int)</code> — a real function — and type-checks it.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Reuse</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Every call with <code>T = int</code> in this translation unit uses the same instantiation; identical instantiations in other TUs are merged by the linker.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Templates are checked twice: syntax when defined, and fully when instantiated. Errors like “no match for <code>operator&gt;</code>” appear only when you use the template with a type that lacks it — which is what <strong>concepts</strong> fix.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-versions\"></i>Overloading and explicit specialisation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstring&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nbool equal(const T&amp; a, const T&amp; b) { return a == b; }\n\ntemplate &lt;&gt;                                               // full specialisation for const char*\nbool equal&lt;const char*&gt;(const char* const&amp; a, const char* const&amp; b) { return std::strcmp(a, b) == 0; }\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nvoid describe(T)  { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"value\\n\"; }\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nvoid describe(T*) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"pointer\\n\"; }           // overload: more specialised, preferred\n\nint main() {\n    const char* x = \"hi\";\n    char buf[] = \"hi\";\n    const char* y = buf;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; equal(1, 1) &lt;&lt; equal(x, y) &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // compares contents, not addresses\n    int n = 0;\n    describe(n);\n    describe(&amp;n);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>11\nvalue\npointer</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Prefer <em>overloading</em> function templates to specialising them — specialisations don't take part in overload resolution, which surprises people.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>Abbreviated templates (C++20)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nauto sum(const auto&amp; container) {          // `auto` parameter = implicit template\n    typename std::decay_t&lt;decltype(container)&gt;::value_type total{};\n    for (const auto&amp; x : container) total += x;\n    return total;\n}\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sum(std::vector&lt;int&gt;{1, 2, 3}) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum(std::vector&lt;double&gt;{0.5, 0.25}) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 0.75</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is template argument deduction?</summary><div><p>The process where the compiler figures out template parameters from the types of the call's arguments, so you can write <code>maxOf(3, 7)</code> instead of <code>maxOf&lt;int&gt;(3, 7)</code>. Deduction doesn't consider implicit conversions: <code>maxOf(3, 7.0)</code> fails because <code>T</code> would have to be both <code>int</code> and <code>double</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Do templates cause code bloat?</summary><div><p>Each distinct instantiation is separate code, so using a template with many types can grow the binary. In exchange each version is specialised and inlinable. Mitigations: factor type-independent code into non-template helpers, and use type erasure where performance isn't critical.</p></div></details></section>"}